From 6177cea0746fe9e707526edbb18915768d458ec1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: HampterDev <131729366+HampterDev@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 16:55:43 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Bound pet and monster talk lines to their buffers
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Three talk-table functions in talk_tables.c wrote client text into fixed
buffers without a bound:

- session_get_pet_speak copied a PetTalkTable.xml line into the caller's
  256-byte buffer with strcpy.
- session_get_monster_talk_info copied the monster's job name into
  name[0x100] with strcpy, and a MonsterTalkTable.xml line into
  line[0x300] with sprintf, using the line as the format.
- session_format_chat_line copied the line into the caller's 256-byte
  out with sprintf, again as the format; replaced the talk words in out
  with strcpy and strcat; and wrote "name : " and the line back into the
  caller's text buffer with sprintf, strcat and strcpy. After 형씨, whose
  replacement 아가씨 is two bytes longer, it took the rest of the line
  from two bytes past the word, which at the end of a line is past its
  NUL.

The three functions now take the size of their output, and their callers
(attack_talk, game_mode_zc_pet_act) pass it. session_format_chat_line
takes the text as const and no longer writes it: it builds the line and
then "name : line" in Strings, and talk_copy copies the result into out
cut by whole characters (text_prefix_length with g_codePage). The talk
words are replaced as before, including the two skipped bytes after
형씨, but the rest is never taken from past the end of the line. The
pet line is cut the same way before it is decoded; the Korean-service
mirror maps lead and trail bytes to lead and trail bytes, so the cut
keeps whole characters. The job name is copied with a bound, and the
monster line is passed to session_format_chat_line directly. Lines are
copied as text, so a % in them now shows.

Validation: the new test_talk_lines runs the extracted functions over
parsed PetTalkTable.xml and MonsterTalkTable.xml, writing into 256-byte
outputs followed by a guard: plain, tabbed, "%s%n%d" and talk-word
lines, the pet's own name and an unknown speaker, a 4-byte output,
300-byte lines, a line whose limit falls inside a CP949 character, a
752-byte monster line and a 319-byte job name. 20 lines; the test fails
on the previous code (run through adapters for the old signatures). A
scratch comparison of the old and new session_format_chat_line on 18
lines that fit, for three speakers and both replacement modes, gave the
same text except "%d", which the old code formatted. The monster-talk
stub of test_attack_records takes the new size and checks it is 0x100.
Release build without warnings.

